import Foundation

extension CapabilityRegistry {
    static let appAndSplits: [CapabilityDefinition] = [
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .bulkSelection, area: "chat list", title: "Several chats at once",
            spec:
                "The chat list can hold a selection and act on all of it: mark rows (space, or the client's own editing mode), mark every row shown or none (⌃a), then delete, archive, save or mark read in one gesture. The set is ChatSelection, keyed on the (profile, session) pair and pruned as the listing moves under it, and the words come from BulkChatCopy so every client names the count the same way. A delete confirms first, naming what goes; rows leave immediately and a partial failure reports what survived — never a silent skip, and never a set that outlives the rows it points at."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .bulkRangeSelection, area: "chat list", title: "Shift-mark selects the span between",
            spec:
                "A mark placed with the shift modifier does not join the set — it re-writes it as the contiguous span the list is drawing between the anchored row and the pressed one, inclusive, the way a file manager reads shift-click. The anchor is the row the last plain mark landed on (ChatSelection.anchorKey, the listed item's lead chat so a split row anchors whole); shift-marking again re-measures the span from that same anchor rather than crawling, and anything that ends the gesture — clearing the set, selecting all, a bulk verb spending it — also forgets the anchor, while a listing change that removes the anchor degrades the next shift-mark to a plain mark. A platform whose pointer cannot express a shift modifier answers notApplicable rather than inventing a second tap gesture nobody asked for."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .bulkSplit, area: "chat list", title: "Marked chats open as one even split",
            spec:
                "A selection can also be spent on the window itself: while several chats are marked, the selection surface offers to open all of them at once as one tiling — side by side, stacked, or as a grid — with every pane an equal share of the window. SplitEven owns the whole gesture: which arrangements a count earns (two chats get the two lines, a third earns the grid, past SplitEven.limit the verbs simply do not appear), the words and glyphs every client names them by, and the equalized tree itself, with panes landing in the order the list was drawing the marked rows. The gesture spends the marks like every other bulk verb and the arrangement persists like any hand-built layout. A platform without tiling answers notApplicable rather than inventing a stack of modal chats."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .splitTabs, area: "chat list", title: "A split is one row, and unsplitting separates it",
            spec:
                "While a window holds several conversations, the chat list draws them as the one thing they are: a single row standing for the whole arrangement, and the moment the window unsplits the rows separate again. The row is a pure function of the window's live layout (SplitTab over the same snapshot the window persists), never a memory of one — a merged row standing for an arrangement that no longer exists would be the list describing a window nobody is looking at, so the row and the panes can never disagree. SplitTabGrouping folds it into the sections every client already builds: the row takes the place of the first of its members, which puts it in the highest section any of its chats earns, and the state it wears is the loudest pane's — a split with a question waiting in one pane is a split that needs you. It is only drawn whole: a listing missing a member cannot honestly say what the window holds, so its surviving chats are drawn plain. Pressing the row goes to the split — it is already on screen, and rebuilding it would tear down the very streams it stands for; pressing a member's own line jumps to the pane showing it; the row's menu offers each chat alone (unsplit down to that one pane) and Unsplit outright (collapse to the focused pane in one gesture, not a close per pane); marking the row holds every chat in it. A platform with no tiling answers notApplicable — there is no window arrangement for a row to stand for."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .keyboardShortcuts, area: "app", title: "The shared shortcut registry",
            spec:
                "Keys resolve through ShortcutSet from ShortcutRegistry.all with ~/.config/tailscode/keybindings.json rebinding, normal/insert/terminal contexts, sequences and conflicts reported."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .shortcutCheatsheet, area: "app", title: "Shortcut cheatsheet",
            spec: "A help overlay derives its content from the effective bindings, config path included."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .shortcutEditor, area: "app", title: "Rebind a shortcut by pressing it",
            spec:
                "Settings has a Keyboard page that lists every action in ShortcutRegistry by category with the keys it answers to now, and changes one by pressing the new keys instead of editing text. ShortcutEditor is the whole arrangement in Core so the desktops cannot disagree: fit() says whether a pressed key may be given to an action at all and as what — a key a text field or a shell would type is kept out of those contexts (a bare n becomes 'only when you are not typing') and refused with the reason where nothing is left; conflicts() names every action that would lose a key, including a two-key sequence whose first key is being taken and a single key that would shadow a new sequence; adding, removing and restoring plan an edit as the complete key list each touched action ends with, and apply writes only those ids through ShortcutFile, which leaves every note, _defaults and unknown id in keybindings.json exactly as it was and refuses to overwrite a file that is not a JSON object. A key that conflicts is never taken silently: the screen names who has it and offers to take it. Every row can be put back to its shipped keys, and so can the whole file. The file stays the source of truth and the way in for a person who prefers text; a change from either side is live without a restart."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .fileBrowser, area: "app", title: "The server's files",
            spec:
                "Browse the conversation directory's tree via FileBrowsingBackend.listFiles; picking a file hands its path to the composer."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .gitState, area: "app", title: "What the repository is doing",
            spec:
                "The repository a conversation is working in, read and never operated: the server answers GET /git with branch, upstream drift, stash count, an operation left half-done, every path the working tree disagrees with the index or HEAD about, and the recent commits, and GitObservingBackend carries it to the client with nil meaning 'this server cannot say' rather than an error. GitState in Core is the whole arrangement — sections in triage order (conflicts, staged, changed, untracked), one row per path with git's own status letter, a symbol, a tone from five meanings and the lines it moved; a header that states the branch, what it owes its upstream and one alert line when a merge or rebase is unfinished; and the chip a chat's chrome wears, which spells the tree out in a prompt's shorthand rather than one lump — ↑↓ for the upstream, ✖ conflicted, + staged, ~ changed, ? untracked, and nothing at all for a mark that would read as zero. A file opens its own diff, read into numbered lines by GitPatchReader so all three clients colour the same gutter, and a commit opens what it changed. No client offers to stage, commit, pull or push: a change made from a phone is a change nobody reviewed on the machine that has to live with it. A tree with no repository says so; a bridge too old for the routes makes the surface disappear rather than fail."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .terminalPane, area: "app", title: "A shell beside the conversation",
            spec:
                "Run shell commands in the conversation's working directory with history, from the same window as the chat."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .splitFromTerminal, area: "app", title: "Split into the terminal's folder",
            spec:
                "The terminal is how a folder is reached, so it is also how a split is aimed: cd there, press the chord (ctrl+shift+o splits right, ctrl+alt+shift+o splits down, from any focus) and the new half is a chat started in the folder the shell is standing in; the existing ctrl+w v and ctrl+w s offer the same folder as the first row of the new pane's chooser (newPaneChooser). The folder is read from the shell itself (the foreground process of its pseudo-terminal), never from the folder the conversation named, so it follows every cd, pushd and jump. The shell is this machine's, so the chat goes to a server that runs on this machine (TerminalSplit.plan): the focused pane's own server when it is local, else the first local one that answers; with none, nothing splits and the refusal says why in words. A terminal that runs one command at a time has no shell standing anywhere between commands and says so rather than splitting into the conversation's own folder. The new half is filled while it is made, on the pane that was split and never on whichever pane the focus wanders to, and it opens focused on its composer."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .listBesideConversation, area: "app", title: "The chat list beside the conversation",
            spec:
                "A window with room for both keeps the list of conversations beside the one that is open, rather than making the list a screen the conversation covers: choosing a row puts that conversation beside the list in place of the last, the row of the conversation on screen stays marked, and the list keeps its scroll, search and filter while conversations change beside it. The list can be put away to give the conversation the whole width and brought back, by the same chord that shows and hides it everywhere. Where a window can be made too narrow for both, the two fold into one stack — the list, and the conversation over it — and widening the window spreads them out again with the same conversation, stream, scroll and unsent words still open; nothing that was open is closed by a resize."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .splitPanes, area: "app", title: "Tiling split panes",
            spec:
                "The conversation surface is a binary tree of live panes (SplitLayout): any pane splits right or down to any depth, and each pane is a complete conversation — its own stream, composer, vim, find, status. The ctrl+w verbs work the tree (directional focus, close, zoom, swap, equalize) and a double click on any divider evens the whole tree out without touching the keyboard, the focused pane wears a visible accent and drives the window chrome, a chat-list row can open into a new split, and the tree with its ratios and sessions persists under tailscode.layout.tree and restores on launch — a pane whose session cannot be found says so instead of collapsing the arrangement. A pane the window has no room for, or one zoomed away, is hidden rather than squeezed: it keeps its place in the tree, is named in the overflow strip, and comes back exactly where it was when there is room again (paneOverflow). And each pane is only as live as its room and the live budget allow — full, glance or parked (paneDensity) — so a crowded window degrades into status tiles instead of slivers."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .newPaneChooser, area: "app", title: "The new pane asks which server",
            spec:
                "A pane with no conversation in it is a chooser, not a caption (PaneChooser): it names every configured server with its address, chat count and whether it is answering, and once one is chosen it offers a new chat there plus that server's recent chats — so a split can land on another machine without touching the chat list. One configured server skips the question; two or more pre-focus the server the pane was already on. Keyboard-first everywhere: arrows or j/k walk, enter opens, 1-9 pick outright, esc steps back to the servers, and the rows are clickable. Choosing New chat here opens the new-chat flow with that server already selected, and the chat it mints opens in the pane that asked. An empty pane is always full density while it is placed — the chooser never becomes a glance or a parked face — and one hidden for want of room shows in the overflow strip like any other pane. Where this client has a shell that stays alive and the chosen server runs on this machine, the first row is New chat in the folder the shell is standing in (PaneChooser.terminalFolder, the same folder splitFromTerminal reads), so ctrl+w v and Enter is a split without a directory browser; the row follows the shell while the question is open, and a pane that is asking owns the keyboard rather than handing it to its composer, whose focus would otherwise swallow every answer key."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .chatDragToPane, area: "app", title: "Drag a chat into a pane",
            spec:
                "A chat-list row is a drag source carrying its profile and session under a private type (PaneDragPayload), never plain text, so a chat dropped on a prompt box cannot arrive as pasted words. Every pane is a drop target: the middle of a pane means open it here, and the outer 28% of any edge means split that pane and give the arriving chat that side (PaneDropTarget/PaneDropZone) — a pane with no room to halve, under 560pt on that axis, only offers to fill. While the pointer is over a pane the arrangement it would make is drawn on top of it, in exactly the region the chat would take, captioned with the verb and the chat's title; leaving the pane or the window takes the highlight with it. The drop opens the chat, focuses the pane it landed in, and persists the tree — an edge drop puts the new pane on the side the highlight promised (SplitLayout.split placingNewFirst), and a drop carrying a chat no listing knows changes nothing. A hidden pane is not a drop target, because nothing of it is under the pointer; a glance or a parked pane is, and a chat dropped into one opens there and promotes it as far as its room and the live budget allow."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .clickToActivate, area: "app", title: "Pressing something activates it",
            spec:
                "Where the pointer goes down is what the keyboard is working in. A press anywhere inside a pane — transcript, prompt box, status band, pill, permission card, chooser, either button — makes that pane the focused one: the accent moves, the window chrome (title, file tree, terminal, remembered session) follows, and the ctrl+w verbs work from there. It happens before the widget under the pointer acts, so a control in a background pane commands its own conversation rather than whichever pane the eye had left behind, and the press keeps its ordinary meaning — nothing is swallowed and keyboard focus lands exactly where the click put it, never grabbed on its behalf. The same rule holds for the regions beside the tree: pressing in the chat list, the file tree or the terminal makes that the keyboard's region, so Tab cycles from where the hand is. A press on a divider, on chrome outside the tree, or in the pane that is already focused changes nothing. A hidden pane activates nothing — it is not on screen to be pressed — and a press on a glance or a parked pane focuses it and promotes it as far as its room and the live budget allow."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .paneDensity, area: "splits", title: "A pane is as alive as its room and the budget allow",
            spec:
                "Every pane has a density — full, glance or parked (PaneDensity) — and the window says how many are live. Density follows geometry first: a chat pane under the full minimum (PaneSizing.chatFull, 280 × 200) is a glance, and it is full again only at 296 × 216 (PaneSizing.allowsFull's 16-point hysteresis, so a drag across the threshold never flickers); under load the governor demotes further but never promotes past what the room allows. A glance is a status tile read from the tail of the conversation — activity face, title, the last words or the question it waits on, model and effort, turn clock, queue and background work — refreshed at the shed level's glance rate with no reveal, no animation and no input field; pressing it focuses and promotes it. A parked pane owns no stream and no clock: its face comes from the list feed, it keeps its last reading dimmed with its age, and Resume brings it back. Hidden, zoomed-away and occluded panes are parked. The window chrome carries one quiet chip saying how many panes are live out of how many and, under load, why; its popover offers Keep all live, which a strained machine ignores and says so. A pane can be kept live (pinned) or paused by hand, and its identity strip wears a Glance, Paused or pin badge — nothing about the state is hidden."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .paneArrangements, area: "splits", title: "Arrangements are shapes, and the tree stays editable",
            spec:
                "The window can be rebuilt into a named arrangement — columns, rows, grid, main and stack (main on the left at SplitEven.mainRatio, the rest stacked at equal heights) or main on top — keeping every pane, its id, the focus, the zoom and the focus history (SplitLayout.arrange over SplitEven.arrange(ids:as:), the same builder a bulk open uses). ctrl+w a walks columns, rows, grid, main and stack (SplitArrangement.cycle) from whatever shape the tree reads as (SplitEven.shape(of:)); ctrl+w return promotes the focused pane into the main slot — the first pane of the root's first side — and promoting the main pane swaps it with the next one and follows it; ctrl+w r and shift+r rotate every pane one slot through the same tree shape; ctrl+w shift+h/j/k/l move the focused pane to that far edge with one pane's fair share. Every verb keeps the tree valid, keeps every surviving pane and the focus on a pane that exists, and the structural ones unzoom exactly as a swap does. Bulk-opening three or more chats offers main and stack beside the lines and the grid, leading from four. The same verbs live in the menu, so none of them needs the keyboard."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .paneOverflow, area: "splits", title: "A pane with no room steps aside and says so",
            spec:
                "Placement is Core's (SplitLayout.placement): a window too small for every pane at its minimum hides the least recently focused panes until the rest fit — never the focused one — and leaves the tree untouched, so growing the window brings them back exactly where they were with every ratio intact, because a solve never writes a ratio. A zoom hides every other pane the same way. Either way a 28-point strip along the bottom of the canvas names each hidden pane as a chip — its activity face, its title cut to 18 characters, a needs-you dot, and +N when the chips overflow — so a crowded or zoomed window still says what the others are doing; pressing a chip focuses its pane, unzooming or swapping it in for the least recently focused placed pane. Hidden panes are parked, are never a press or drop target, and are skipped by cycling."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .paneResizeByKey, area: "splits", title: "Dividers move from the keyboard and say where they are",
            spec:
                "Every divider can be moved without the pointer: ctrl+w > < + - make the focused pane wider, narrower, taller or shorter by 16 points (SplitLayout.resize, over the nearest divider bounding it on that axis), and a focused divider answers the arrow keys — 16 points, 64 with shift — and Home and End for its extremes. Every move, key or pointer, goes through SplitLayout.drag, which clamps the divider to the range where both sides keep their minimum (DividerPlacement.lowest and highest), so no key and no gesture squeezes a pane below its minimum and both desktops move by the same arithmetic. A divider is an accessible splitter — labelled with the two panes it divides, carrying its position as a value between its extremes, stepped by the same nudge — and a double click still evens the tree out."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .paneRearrange, area: "splits", title: "A pane moves by dragging its strip",
            spec:
                "A pane's identity strip is a drag source carrying the pane under a private type (PaneMovePayload, application/x-tailscode-pane) — never plain text, and never mistaken for a chat. Over another pane it is aimed with exactly the zones a dragged chat uses (PaneDropTarget.zone): the middle swaps the two panes, an edge moves the dragged pane to that side of the target (PaneDropTarget.move, then SplitLayout.apply), and the highlight names which with the zone's move verb before anything happens. A pane dropped on itself, on chrome or outside the window changes nothing; a move focuses the pane that moved and persists the tree."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .safeRestore, area: "splits", title: "A crash does not relaunch into the load that caused it",
            spec:
                "Every launch records that it has not yet exited cleanly and every exit path records that it did; what to restore is a pure decision over that ledger and the snapshot (RestorePlan). After a clean exit the arrangement comes back at once and its panes wake staggered — the focused one first, then the most recently focused, 300 ms apart — so a restore never opens every stream in one frame. After an unclean exit with three or more panes the shape comes back with every chat parked and a non-modal banner over the canvas saying the app did not close normally and how many chats are paused, offering Resume all and Resume one by one; a second unclean exit in a row also holds the shed level at loaded for ten minutes. One or two panes restore normally. A restore never waits on the network to draw the window's shape, and a snapshot of more than twelve panes keeps the first twelve in reading order."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .flightRecorder, area: "splits", title: "The app keeps a black box",
            spec:
                "A fixed ring of 192-byte records on disk, written once a second and at once on a shed-level change or a stall, holds what the next freeze needs to be explained: memory, threads, descriptors, panes by density, shed level, main-loop busy ratio and worst stall, deepest mailbox, drain time, memory pressure and available memory, divider relayout time and an event word. Counts and durations only — never a title, an id, a path or a word of a conversation. A hard freeze loses at most a second, the launch record names the app and toolkit versions and the renderer, --flight prints the ring decoded with the newest last, and Settings offers to copy it."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .resourceGuard, area: "splits", title: "The app cannot take the machine down with it",
            spec:
                "Where the operating system allows, the app bounds itself at launch: a launch into the app's own systemd scope sets MemoryHigh, MemoryMax, MemorySwapMax=0, CPUWeight and TasksMax on that scope (clamped fractions of RAM), leaves an administrator's own limit alone, never touches a terminal's scope, honours TAILSCODE_NO_LIMITS=1, and --limits prints what was applied and why. Everywhere the app watches its own pressure — main-loop busy ratio, stalls through a watchdog thread, host memory pressure, its own footprint against its limit, thermal state and low power where readable — and sheds in levels (TileGovernor): fewer live panes, slower glances, the cascade and animations off, caches emptied through MemoryRelief, with the reason in the window's live chip. What a platform cannot do — no cgroups on the Mac, no user manager inside a Flatpak — is stated, not hidden."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .pointerFeedback, area: "app",
            title: "What can be pressed answers the pointer before the press",
            spec:
                "A window where nothing answers until it is clicked reads slower than it is, and a click on something that gave no sign it would take one lands as a surprise. So everything pressable answers a pointer resting on it: a chat row, a thought's or a tool call's header, an agent's card, a picture and every button come up on a plate of the platform's own ink at low strength — never a palette colour, because a hover is neither motion nor affirmation and it lands on glass as often as on the canvas — deepen the moment the button goes down, and act when it comes up over the thing pressed, never when a press dragged away is let go. On a desk that fronts one window at a time, the first click on a window that is not in front lands on what it was aimed at rather than only bringing the window forward, for anything that destroys nothing. A chat row the pointer rests on trades its age for its commonest verbs — pin, save, archive and its own menu — and a message offers its own with the time it was written: Copy, which is the words as written without the thoughts, and, on a prompt a server can wind back to, Undo from here through the same confirmation. A list is watched through one tracking area rather than one per row, so the pointer costs a lookup per move."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .uiScale, area: "app", title: "Type scale",
            spec:
                "Reading size is adjustable, and on the desktops it is adjustable where it matters: TypeArea names the five parts of the window whose type a person can resize on its own (the chat list and pills, what you type, what the agent writes back, code and tools, the terminal — the desktops that predate the split keep one prose size, under the key the agent's words now keep), each a percent of normal from 60 to 250 in steps of five (TypeScale, persisted per area under tailscode.scale.<area> by TypeScaleStore, absent at normal so a reset leaves nothing behind). Settings shows a preview drawn from the same roles the window is drawn from, so a slider is seen before it is trusted, offers presets for a person who wants bigger type and not four decisions (TypePreset: default, comfortable, large, bigger reading, bigger code, with Custom said when the sizes match none), puts a way back at every level (an area to normal, the whole set to the default) and applies as the hand pauses rather than at an OK. The window zoom (⌘+ ⌘− ⌘0 on the Mac, under tailscode.uiScale) stacks on top of the areas. A phone answers with the platform's own type system (Dynamic Type)."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .typeRamp, area: "app", title: "Type ramp",
            spec:
                "Every place the app sets type names a TypeRole from Core's shared ramp (Typography.spec) and no client invents a size, a weight, a tracking or a leading of its own. A role carries the axis it scales with (chrome, prose, mono), the face it is set in — prose, mono, or canvas, which is the client's own voice for the two things a person types — and the four values a client would otherwise guess. The ramp exists to make type say what an accent rule was saying alone: the prompt is the heavier voice and the answer the lighter one with the leading, because the question is a heading over its own answer; a name outweighs its detail inside a row (a tool's name is semibold, its arguments are not); tracking is spent only on small labels that would clot and display numbers that would sprawl; and anything that changes while a person watches it is set in tabular figures, so a settled number never reads as motion. TypographyTests proves the claims, so a client that drifts fails the build rather than the eye."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .themePicker, area: "app", title: "Theme",
            spec: "One catalog of named themes (AppTheme.all in Core), chosen on every client and persisted under tailscode.theme, with light or dark pinned separately under tailscode.appearance because picking Gruvbox should not also decide whether it is night. Each theme carries its own two appearances and follows the system between them unless pinned; every slot is published through Palette.corrected(), which walks lightness in OKLab until the colour clears WCAG on its own canvas, so a theme that cannot be made readable fails the build (ThemeTests). The slots are meanings, not decorations — accent is motion, warn is attention, danger is failure, info is what the agent touched, special is a standing mark — and no client may spend one on a second meaning. Where the client paints every pixel (Linux GTK) the palette is the whole window. Where the OS owns the materials (iOS and macOS Liquid Glass) the palette owns the content layer only and the glass is left alone to drink from it: no bar, sidebar or glass surface takes a palette background, ink that sits on glass stays a system colour so it flips with the material, and the palette reaches the chrome through the app tint and through the canvas the chrome floats over. Those two clients also offer System — the platform's own colours, and their default — because an app whose materials Apple drew has a real answer to give when someone wants no theme at all."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .settingsSurface, area: "app", title: "Settings",
            spec: "App preferences live on one discoverable surface, persisted under the shared tailscode.* keys."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .goalControl, area: "app", title: "Goals are visible and settable",
            spec:
                "ConversationState.goal is shown when set, and the user can set/clear it from the conversation surface."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .firstRunSetup, area: "app", title: "First run is a verified checklist",
            spec:
                "Setup states each requirement and proves what it can — this machine's tailnet presence, and which agent answers the typed address via probeCandidates — before asking for anything."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .emptyWelcome, area: "app", title: "An empty window is a welcome",
            spec:
                "When the app holds no real server (the demo's are not one) the main pane draws EmptyWelcome instead of a blank transcript: a title, one sentence, the next step read from the same FirstRunPlan the first-run window draws (Tailscale not running shows the Tailscale row's own remedy, a missing machine shows the machine row, a signed-out Claude shows the sign-in) and three actions with one primary: Set up a machine opens first run, Paste a link opens it on the link way, Try the demo enters the demo. The sidebar's empty list carries the same primary button and EmptyWelcome.sidebarLine. Every word is Core's; a client decides only how it is drawn, and the welcome goes away the moment a real server is saved."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .addAnotherMachine, area: "servers", title: "Adding a machine is first run's job, everywhere",
            spec:
                "The servers screen has no form of its own for adding a machine. Add a machine opens the first-run checklist in its addition mode (FirstRunState.Mode.addition: no Tailscale row, no demo, no Later, so it never claims Tailscale is missing when a machine is already connected), which is the one place the link way, the install-command way and the tailnet scan exist; a copy that cannot scan the tailnet says so once and leads with the link. A link on the pasteboard is offered, never used: while the checklist is the key window and the clipboard holds a parseable ConnectLink the machine step shows Use the link you copied with the machine's name and place and never its key, which stays redacted and held back until Connect (FirstRunPlan.offer). A failed probe on the servers screen is ConnectDiagnosis with the one tap that fixes it, never a hand-written sentence. When first run or an addition ends with a live server the window closes, a toast names the machine that connected (FirstRunLanding) and the active pane opens PaneChooser on that machine's own step, so the person lands where a conversation starts."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .tailscaleReadiness, area: "app",
            title: "Not on the tailnet is four states, each with its own fix",
            spec:
                "Whether this device is on a tailnet is a TailscaleReading rather than an address or nothing: up, signed out, service not running, not installed, or — inside a sandbox — unable to see. Each carries what is true, why it matters and the one action that fixes it, and the client wires that action to its own door: the download page, a sign-in that surfaces the login URL where the person is already looking, or the platform's own Tailscale. Nobody is told to open a terminal, and 'this app cannot see' is never drawn as 'you are not connected'."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .demoMode, area: "app", title: "Try it without a server",
            spec:
                "First run offers a scripted demo world (MockBackend) so the product can make its argument before any machine is set up — a real conversation surface over fake servers, clearly labeled, exitable back to setup."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .hapticFeedback, area: "app", title: "Cues you feel, at a strength you choose",
            spec:
                "Every physical cue is a named HapticCue rather than an amplitude at the call site, played from HapticRecipe's composed pattern. The cues that carry a turn — sent, progress, needs-you, finished — are the point: waiting on another machine is what the app asks of someone, and the wait is reported to the hand. Every recipe is authored to be pleasant at full strength (rounded body, soft attack, at most one reinforcing beat, low sharpness), one setting scales all of them through HapticStrength.drive, reinforcement drops out below its floor, and repeats inside minimumGap are dropped so a burst of tool calls reads as progress rather than a rattle. Hardware that cannot compose falls back to canned feedback that keeps each cue's meaning. The setting is chosen by feel: the control plays every stop it passes at the strength under the finger."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .activityNotifications, area: "app", title: "The app taps your shoulder",
            spec:
                "A turn ending or a needs-you state in an unfocused session raises a system notification that deep-links back to it, wearing the same face the chat's status badge raises so a stack of them separates the finished from the waiting before a word is read."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .missedActivity, area: "app", title: "What happened while you were away",
            spec:
                "A notification is a thing that appears for seconds and is then gone whether or not anybody saw it, so every alert the client raises is also written down in ActivityInbox and listed until it is looked at: what happened, in which chat, on which server, how long ago, with the ones still blocking a turn first. Opening the chat clears its own entries — a glance at the list is not looking, or the list would empty itself as it was drawn — and so does reading it on any other device, because the read mark travels through the servers and the finished-news for a chat that is no longer unread is settled by the same listing that carries it (ActivityInbox.isRead, held back for a short lag so a listing from before the turn ended cannot settle news it has not heard of; a question or an approval is never settled by a look), and an approval or question answered on the server leaves the list by the same withdrawal that takes the notification back, so it can never send someone to a chat that is waiting on nothing. The list offers to clear itself whole — which reads the chats behind the finished news it clears, so the dismissal reaches every other device through the read mark — and says nothing at all when there is nothing to say. A notice names its chat by the name a person would recognise: the first turn is the news and the server has not named it yet, so MissedActivity.name falls back to the words that started it, and ActivityInbox.reconcile holds the standing list against every fresh listing — adopting the name the server has since written, dropping a chat that was deleted on a server that answered, and dropping finished-news for a session that is working again, while a server that said nothing is never read as having deleted anything."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .liveActivity, area: "app", title: "A turn you can follow with the phone locked",
            spec:
                "A turn this device starts is followed on the Lock Screen and in the Dynamic Island, and the card says what the chat's own status surface says — thinking, writing, the tool out on the machine wearing that tool's own symbol, compacting, and a turn stopped for the person, which leads every other card — with the turn's clock beside it. The words are named rather than written: the app and claude-bridge both stamp a LiveActivityDetail and the facts it is made from, and the widget writes the words in the reader's language, so a card a server pushed while the phone slept reads exactly as well as one the app drew. An ending is news until it has been read, so a card never vanishes when the work stops: it settles in place — how the turn ended (done, with what it did and what the machine is still carrying for it; the question it stopped to ask; a failure; a cut-off; an answer that came back empty), how long it ran and how long ago it ended — and stays in the Dynamic Island and on the Lock Screen until the conversation is opened, which is what reads it. A turn that ends under the reader's eyes leaves nothing behind. The conversation's next turn takes the same card back rather than stacking a second one, whether this device sent it, a queued message drained, or the machine began one itself, which the server announces by push. A card nobody came for leaves the Dynamic Island after LiveActivityLinger.island and the Lock Screen after LiveActivityLinger.lockScreen, on the device's clock and the server's alike; a relaunch adopts the cards already standing instead of sweeping them away; and several at once rank by what they say — waiting on the person, then moving, then gone wrong, then finished."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .turnEndWhileAway, area: "app", title: "A turn that ends while the app is closed still says so",
            spec:
                "A turn a person started can outlast the seconds the phone stays in front, and the owner decided this app never leans on a relay for that news — Midgar's own push key is a thing only the first-party bridge holds, so a relay would tell an App Store person nothing while quietly telling everyone else. So the phone itself holds a side-effect-free, fully authorised, idempotent wait open on the server directly, arms it while still in front, and asks nothing of any address in between. When the wait resolves — the turn ended, or it stopped to ask the person something — the phone that is woken for it posts the same local notification the in-app path would have posted, settles the Live Activity with the same LiveActivityDetail the in-app path would have written, and records the same MissedActivity entry, so a turn answered from the background reads exactly like one watched live rather than through a second, thinner vocabulary. Nothing here claims more than the mechanism can promise: Background App Refresh being off, or the app being force-quit, or the OS never scheduling a discretionary task, all mean silence until the app is opened, and the words that explain the capability say exactly that rather than promising a wake the platform reserves the right to skip."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .homeQuickActions, area: "app", title: "Home screen quick actions",
            spec:
                "Long-pressing the app icon offers a jump list: New Chat focuses the composer, Saved chats opens the device-local bookmarks, Usage opens the quota panel, Quick Ask summons the question composer, and a dynamic Resume item tracks the most recent session and opens it. The jump list is for what someone reaches for daily rather than what they did once — adding a server is setup, and setup belongs inside the app. Every action lands on the same destination as its in-app tap, and one that arrives before the main UI exists (a cold launch, or before any server is set up) is parked and delivered the moment Home appears."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .usageWidgets, area: "app", title: "The quotas without opening the app",
            spec:
                "Where the platform lets an app put a reading outside itself, the account's quotas are readable without launching anything: every widget family, the Lock Screen accessories, and a Control Center button, all rendered from the shared snapshot by one reading (WidgetGlance) so a phone, a lock screen and a control can never disagree. The reading is the whole contract and no client invents a word of it: windows ranked with the walls first and the tightest next, a full window drawn as the state it is rather than as 100%, money with no ceiling drawn as a balance rather than as a bar at zero, an empty balance ranked as the wall it is, and a snapshot nobody could refresh saying so before it says anything else — a percentage presented as current is the one lie this surface can tell. Each size is given only the rows it can hold and states what it left out rather than truncating silently, and every row carries its own sentence for a reader who gets no columns. It is configurable in the platform's own editor rather than by a rebuild: which providers (read from the snapshot, so a provider the account actually has is offered and one that stopped answering is not silently unpicked), how the rows are chosen, how much each row says, where the colour comes from, and whether the reset clock is shown. The colour follows the app's own theme through a mirror in the shared container — an extension cannot see the app's defaults, and a Home Screen wearing a different palette than the app it opens reads as two apps — while meaning always outranks branding: a wall is the failure colour whatever else was picked, and a tinted or Lock Screen rendering flattens to one ink without losing a fact. The extension refreshes itself inside its own budget rather than waiting for the app to be opened, and a fetch that fails serves the stored snapshot rather than an error."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .presenceOrb, area: "app", title: "A presence with a body (alpha)",
            spec:
                "The aggregate of everything this device watches, worn as one small GPU-rendered creature on the app's main surface — off by default, opt-in from settings under the shared tailscode.presenceOrb key (PresenceOrbSetting), and labeled alpha wherever it is offered. The whole behaviour is the shared simulation: PresenceSignal.aggregate distils every conversation's ActivityKind to one tone, one motion and a count, and PresenceField turns that into the frame a client rasterises with a shader and adds nothing to — a breathing core, one satellite per open turn emerging onto its own slow orbit, colour from the palette's own tone slots (the platform's colours under System). The mapping is the meaning and it obeys the activity doctrine: work breathes on the shared swell, a turn waiting on the person knocks twice on the attention tone, a failure holds the danger colour perfectly still — a broken thing is never animated into looking busy, or cute — and idle is a dim body at rest. Ultracode rims it with the shared rainbow on the shared aura clock. Every parameter approaches its target on a short time constant, from absolute time, so a state may change in a frame but the body always arrives. When a frame reports itself settled the client stops its clock and the GPU goes quiet; reduced motion shows each state's resting arrangement and loses nothing else. Touching the orb opens the conversation that most needs the person, and a screen reader gets the whole state in words (PresenceSignal.spoken)."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .auroraStream, area: "chat", title: "The answer written by the GPU (alpha)",
            spec:
                "The cascade is one effect with two rasterisers, and which one writes is a choice under the shared tailscode.streamRenderer key (StreamRendererSetting) — classic by default, labeled alpha wherever it is offered, and chosen on a screen that runs both hands side by side on the same sentence rather than describing them, because a reveal cannot be judged from a percentage any more than a haptic can. Nothing about the meaning changes between them: StreamCascade still decides every glyph's heat, the band over it and how far it has entered, the pacer still plays out of the same buffer at the same speed, and the gate still holds the renderer at the last position where no inline token is half-open. What the alpha renderer adds is what an attributed string cannot hold — AuroraField, the same distance-behind-the-edge arithmetic spent as geometry: ink that lands rather than appears, each glyph settling the last fraction of its own height with its own seeded tilt, colour channels pulled apart at the edge and closing behind it, light spilling off a hot letterform onto the page, embers that die well inside the wave because nothing may still be moving where the text has settled, and a nib riding the leading edge that burns with the pacer's own rate and rests rather than goes out when a turn stops to run something. The per-frame cost is the point: geometry is rebuilt when text arrives, and between arrivals a frame is one uniform buffer and one draw call however long the answer is, on the display's own clock at up to 120Hz. Every constant is Core's and asserted by AuroraTests, so a shader that drifts fails a test rather than merely looking different; the client resolves the theme's inks, reads the display's clock, and adds nothing. It degrades rather than breaks: no GPU, reduced motion, a row the geometry cannot be read from, or a burst past the tuning all fall back to the settled renderer for that row, and the settled renderer remains the whole product's floor."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .browserSlot, area: "splits", title: "Browser slot in the split grid",
            spec:
                "A pane can hold a web page. WebTarget reads an address, a bare host, a port on this machine or words to look up; the page renders inside the grid with the platform's own engine, and every split verb treats it like any other pane. The slot asks for an address when it is empty, wears the page's own title in its identity strip, and claims only the chords a browser owns (ctrl+l, ctrl+r, alt+←→, ctrl+±/0, esc) so every other key belongs to the page."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .imageGenSlot, area: "splits", title: "Draw slot in the split grid",
            spec:
                "A pane can paint. ImageGenEndpoint names the ComfyUI server that does the work, ImageGenSlot holds the ask — words, an engine (Qwen for quality, Klein for speed), an aspect, and either a blank canvas or a picture handed in to edit — and the slot queues the render, watches it, and keeps every picture it finished, newest first, each one captioned with the words and the seconds that made it. The pane is a pane like any other: it splits, the dividers resize it, zoom hides its siblings, closing it hands the space back, and the layout snapshot restores the endpoint and the last prompt. An empty slot asks what to draw with the chips already showing, a painting one shows its progress in its own body, and a finished one puts the picture where the work is — beside the conversation that asked for it. What it costs is stated rather than discovered: the render runs on the machine ComfyUI runs on, not in this pane's grid."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .livePreview, area: "splits", title: "Live preview pane",
            spec:
                "A pane can hold a running iOS app. PreviewTarget names an app folder on a Mac that runs a preview host (previewd, port 4300), and PreviewRunner builds it on that Mac's simulator and streams the screen as it changes, so the app is looked at beside the conversation that is writing it. The pane is a pane like any other: it splits, the dividers resize it, zoom hides its siblings, closing it hands the space back, and the layout snapshot restores the same app on the same Mac. It wears PreviewLight in its identity strip — starting and reconnecting sweep, a change being shown breathes, a Mac that needs setting up knocks, and live, broken and offline hold perfectly still — and until the Mac is ready it draws the host's own checklist in PreviewReading's words rather than a blank pane. When a change would not build, the last picture that worked stays on screen with the failure under it, so a mistake costs words and never the view."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .previewLenses, area: "splits", title: "Lenses over the running app",
            spec:
                "A preview pane reads the running app more than one way. PreviewLens names six — Look (the picture), Compare (two shots of one screen with the changed places outlined), Inspect (point at a view and the app's own tree says what it is), Quality (the findings of an audit of the screen), Matrix (the same screen under other sizes and settings) and Rewind (the last ten seconds, with the slow frames marked) — and a plain digit picks one while the pane has the keyboard. Each lens asks the host only when it is opened and everything it started ends when it is left or the pane is parked, so a lens nobody is looking at costs nothing; a host too old for a lens says so in words rather than showing an empty stage."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .previewTouch, area: "splits", title: "Operate the running app from the picture",
            spec:
                "When the preview host reports that it can touch the simulator (PreviewState.acceptsTouch), the picture is operable. A press and release on it is a tap, a drag is a swipe sent on release (PreviewTouchTracker decides which), the wheel and the touchpad become the swipes a finger would make (PreviewScrollAccumulator: one currency for notches and smooth deltas, at most one swipe every 120 ms, none lost), and a Home button sends the home key. A Keys switch forwards typed characters, Return and Delete to the app's focused field (PreviewKeyReading), batches a burst of characters into one request, lets Escape give the keyboard back, and turns itself off when the pane leaves the Look lens. The host can only show a touch once it has acted, so the client answers at once and honestly (PreviewTouchFeedback): a ring opens at the press, turns to a calm busy ring if the host is still working on it, and fades when the touch is delivered or has failed, and a drag leaves a fading line; reduced motion keeps the ring still and changes only its light. Touches go to the host one at a time in the order they were made (PreviewTouchQueue joins neighbours that say one thing and gives up the oldest scroll of a runaway queue first), never on the interface thread, and a refusal is said in PreviewTouchReading's words over the picture, never in a dialog. Whether the pointer operates the app or only watches is the person's choice (PreviewTouchPreference), on by default and remembered per device; Inspect keeps its own pointer and a press there pins a view and never taps."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .imageLane, area: "composer", title: "A picture is a lane on the prompt box",
            spec:
                "A picture is something a person asks for in words, so it is asked for where words are typed rather than only in a pane somebody has to know how to open. `QuickAskLane.image` is a lane on the one composer every client already has — its own placeholder, the video lane's own send word (Render, because it spends the same machine's card), its own chips (`QuickAskLane.imageChips` over `ImageGenField`, which is also what the studio walks, so the composer and the surface can never disagree about what a picture is made from) and its own sentence for a screen reader. Which lanes exist is not a setting and there is nothing to switch on: `QuickAskLane.offered(imaging:)` answers it from whether this device knows a machine that can paint, and `ImageGenDoor` decides that without asking anybody — an address filed for pictures, else the video renderer's own machine, because one ComfyUI holds both sets of models and pointing at it once is the whole of pointing at it. So the lane arrives the moment a renderer does, in every composer at once and with no restart, and leaves with it (`ForgeStore.didChange`/`ImageGenStore.didChange`). It never blinks out underneath somebody: ComfyUI is socket-activated and stops itself when idle, so silence from a machine that has been filed is a machine asleep rather than a machine gone — the door opens on an address existing, and what the machine last answered (`ImageGenSighting`, recorded by whoever looked) decides what the lane *says* rather than whether it is there. The words are Core's: `ImageGenDoor.line` names the inherited machine, a machine that was not answering when it was last checked, or model files that are missing, and says nothing at all about a machine nobody has looked at yet, because a guess dressed as a fact is the one thing this vocabulary refuses. Pressing it opens the same shape the forge opens (`ImageGenSurface`, `ImageGenEntryPoint`): a modal over the window rather than a pane beside it — making a picture is a task you start, watch and collect, not a place you work, and a surface nobody types into while it runs does not earn half a transcript's width or a pane left behind afterwards. The conversation is untouched behind it and comes back whole. Closing may never stop the render: the job lives above the surface, the surface says so while one is out (`ImageGenSurface.dismissNote`), and reopening finds the same picture exactly where it was. The wait is narrated by the machine, not guessed: the runner follows the render on ComfyUI's own socket under a client id minted once per device (`ImageGenStore.clientID`), and `ImageGenProgress` turns each frame into words — waiting behind N renders, loading the model, reading the reference, reading the words, painting step k of n, decoding, saving — with a bar drawn only from the sampler's own count and never sitting at zero; history still decides the outcome, so a socket that drops costs words and nothing else. Stop takes the render off the machine the precise way — deleted from the queue if it has not started, interrupted if it has (`ImageGenClient.stop`) — rather than only ceasing to watch. A machine is checked file by file (`ImageGenModelFile`, `ImageGenSighting.available(_:)`): a box dressed for Klein and not for Qwen paints fast pictures today and says so in the lane's line, the engine chip wears the warning and its menu names what is missing, and a send that would fail is refused in words before the queue error would have said it worse (`ImageGenWords.cannotRender`); the whole account — address, version, last check, queue, each of the six files present or missing, check again, change machine — is one sheet (`ImageGenMachineWords`) reached from the studio's own bar. Fast edits: Klein's verified reference graph (`ImageGenClient.kleinEditGraph`) scales the picture to a megapixel and takes its size as the canvas, so an edit no longer paints from words alone while the button says Edit; while a reference is held the aspect chip stands down (`ImageGenSlot.aspectApplies`), because both editors take the size from the picture. A reference reaches the studio through five doors (`ImageGenReferenceSource`: photo library, camera, a file, the clipboard, the machine's own gallery) from the studio and from the composer's lane alike, the chip wears the picture it holds and offers replace or remove, and a finished picture's verbs are `ImageGenAction.offered` — save, share, copy, the same words again, start from this — every one available from the stage and from a press-and-hold on any tile."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .imageLibrary, area: "composer", title: "Pictures the machine keeps",
            spec:
                "Every render ComfyUI makes lands in one output directory on the machine, whoever asked for it — this phone, a desk, the machine's own browser — and that directory is the durable account of what has been painted there, so the studio shows it as a gallery rather than only the pictures this device happens to remember. The listing is the machine's own (`GET /internal/files/output`, newest first) read through `ImageGenClient.listOutputs`; a picture just made is the newest entry in it, matched by the name the machine gave the file (`ImageGenPicture.remoteName`), so the session and the gallery are one shelf rather than two. Nothing heavy travels for a tile: the machine re-encodes a small copy on request (`/view?preview=`), and what a picture *is* — its size, its day, its pixel dimensions and the words, seed, steps and model that made it — is read from the head of the file itself in one ranged request (`PNGHead`, `ComfyRecipe`: ComfyUI stamps the graph into every PNG it writes, and the prompt is found by walking the sampler's positive wire back to the node holding words, so a picture made from somebody's own graph reads back as well as this app's). What was learned is kept on the device (`ImageGenLibraryCache`: listing, facts, thumbnails, a bounded set of originals) so the second opening costs nothing and a machine asleep still has a gallery, marked as of when. Putting a kept picture on the stage gives it the same caption, facts and verbs as one made here — save, share, copy, open, the same words again where the file recorded them, and start the next render from it, which names the file in the machine's own directory (`LoadImage` reads `name [output]`) so no byte travels — and never discard, because the folder is the machine's. A server too old for the listing route says so (`ImageGenLibraryFailure.unsupported`) rather than showing an empty shelf, and a file with no graph in it is a picture with no words rather than a row of zeros."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .imagePromptHelper, area: "composer", title: "A small model writes the paragraph",
            spec:
                "Qwen Image was trained behind a rewriter, so three words in gets a picture of three words; the studio therefore offers to have the brief written out by a language model on the same tailnet, and the whole of that offer is Core's. Which model writes is a choice the person sees and makes: `ImageGenHelperFinder.survey` asks every OpenAI-shaped door near the painting machine and on this device at once (Ollama, llama-swap, llama.cpp, LM Studio, vLLM) and answers with every machine that replied and every model it serves (`ImageGenHelperServer`, `ImageGenHelperModel` — the endpoint's own display name, the id, and whether it is in memory), and the picker beside the Enhance control lists them grouped by machine with the current one marked, so a bigger writer on a second port is never hidden behind the first port that answered. The default is the best writer rather than the fastest (`ImageGenHelperFinder.preferred`: size class first, thinking models and embedders never, a loaded model to break a tie), remembered as `ImageGenHelper` with the name it was listed under (`ImageGenStore.helper`), and switchable off without being forgotten. A rewrite is watched rather than waited for: `ImageGenRewriter` streams the paragraph as it is written (`ImageGenEnhancer.stream`, the JSON the model is asked for decoded a token at a time by `ImageGenBrief.partialExpansion`, thinking stripped) into a card (`ImageGenRewriteDraft`) under the words, headed by who is writing and how many words so far, and lands with exactly three verbs — use these words, keep mine, write again — plus one line for what to change, which sends the same paragraph back for a revision (`ImageGenRewriteContext.instruction`). The helper is told what the brief alone does not say: which engine paints, the shape the person chose by hand (kept, and answered back) or none (the helper chooses and the chip follows), how many reference pictures the words address and that their `<imageN>` tokens must stay, and the avoid list it must leave out. Taking the paragraph puts it in the box where it can still be edited and keeps the typed sentence one press away (undo); nothing is ever sent on the person's behalf. Every word is `ImageGenRewriteWords`, every state has a face, and a machine with no helper says what would do (`noneFoundHint`) rather than failing quietly."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .imageLivePreview, area: "composer", title: "The picture is watched as it is painted",
            spec:
                "A render is a minute of somebody's card, and a pulse over a blank tells nothing about whether that minute is going right. ComfyUI decodes the sampler's latent after every step and sends the sketch down the same socket the progress comes on, as a binary frame; `ImageGenPreviewFrame.read` reads it (event 1, and the metadata-fronted event 4), `ImageGenRunner` hands each frame to the surface beside the progress it already reports, and the stage draws the sketch in place of the pulse — full width, the newest frame replacing the last without a rebuild, captioned as a sketch with the sampler's own step (`ImageGenPreviewWords`) so it is never mistaken for the picture. The frame changes only the paintable, never the layout around it; the finished picture arrives under the last sketch — a crossfade once, from the sketch to the picture, with the sketch let go when it is over — rather than in place of it, and the verbs the picture will land with already hold their room while it is painted so the stage does not shrink by a row as it arrives; reduced motion shows the picture at once; a stop or a failure clears the sketch; and a server started without `--preview-method` sends no frames, in which case the stage shows exactly what it showed before and nothing is inferred. The sketch is the machine's, never re-encoded or kept: it is not saved, not offered, and not on the shelf."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .studioSheet, area: "composer", title: "The Studio rises inside the window",
            spec:
                "Making a picture or a clip is a task you start, watch and collect, so the Studio is part of the window it was opened from rather than a second one to find, bury or lose. It rises as a sheet: 24 points in from the sides, clear of the title bar so the traffic lights and the chat's name stay in view, flush with the bottom edge, the conversation dimmed behind it by a plain scrim that never blurs and closes the sheet when pressed (StudioSheetGeometry for the rectangle, StudioSheetMotion for every duration, distance and alpha — 38 % dark, 26 % light — and the quiet hairline and upward shadow that give its edge). One motion carries it in and the same motion out, and only reduced motion changes it, to a short cross-fade: the sheet is opaque from its first frame, its content is laid out at its final size before it moves and is moved as one layer, and nothing inside re-measures per frame. StudioSheetState is the one state machine — closed, opening, open, closing — and a Studio asked for while it is up changes lane without animating. The keyboard belongs to the sheet from the first frame of its rise and goes back to the conversation the moment it starts to leave (StudioSheetState.capturesKeys, conversationChordsEnabled): every chord that acts on the chat behind it is disabled while it is up. Esc stops a render that is out and only the next Esc closes (StudioSheetKeys.escape), and the Stop control and Done say so in words (StudioSheetWords.escapeHint); Command-W on the Mac and Ctrl+W on Linux close the sheet before the window (StudioSheetKeys.closes). Closing never stops a render: the job lives above the surface. The Mac hosts the Studio's two lanes behind a lane switch; Linux hosts both in the one sheet instead of two windows, which is also what lets every dialog the Studio opens be an ordinary transient of the one window. Where a client has moved its media viewer onto the sheet — the Mac has — the gallery of the conversation's pictures and a clip's player are a second content of the same host rather than a window of their own: a lights-down canvas in both faces, the viewer's own 44-point toolbar row, and a sheet opened from the Studio stacks StudioSheetMetrics.stackInset further in on the top and both sides (StudioSheetGeometry's depth) so the Studio's edge shows above it, at most StudioSheetMetrics.maximumDepth deep; Esc, the close chord, Done and a press on the scrim close the top sheet only, every conversation chord stays off, and the Studio's own chords are off while a viewer is on it. A phone has no window to be inside, so iOS keeps the full-screen modal it has always had, and an iPad uses the same."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoSlot, area: "splits", title: "Video slot in the split grid",
            spec:
                "A pane can hold a stream instead of a chat. VideoTarget reads a channel, a link or words; the slot plays it inside the grid, and every split verb treats it like any other pane — it splits, the dividers resize it, zoom hides its siblings, closing it hands the space back, and the layout snapshot restores what it was watching. An empty slot asks what to watch, a playing one states the stream's own title in its identity strip, and VideoCommand answers space/m/e/arrows without taking keys from the chat panes. What it costs the grid is stated rather than discovered: VideoNotice's line rides the chooser row that offers a stream and the empty slot's own body, and a playing slot keeps the short of it at the end of its identity strip until it is paused."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .watchAccounts, area: "splits", title: "Signing in to Twitch and YouTube",
            spec:
                "Signing in is what lets the board list the channels somebody already follows, and it is never a condition of the board working. Both sites are entered through the OAuth device flow (WatchSignIn, toolkit-free): the app asks the site for a short code, the person confirms it in their own browser, and no password is ever typed into this app — the tokens land in whatever the platform gave the app for secrets (Keychain on Apple, the 0600 file store on Linux) as one blob per site, and the display name lands in ordinary settings so a row can draw itself without unlocking anything. Twitch needs no registered application because its own web client's device flow accepts the same client id the signed-out directory already reads with; YouTube will only answer a registered app, so a build given none says exactly what is missing and how to supply it rather than offering a button that fails. Signed in, Twitch answers every followed channel's live state in one request and YouTube answers who is subscribed while the free path still answers which of them are live — quota is never spent on a question the signed-out board already answers. A settings section (WatchAccounts.rows) states each site as a fact first: the account when there is one, what signing in would add when there is not. An account that expires past refreshing signs itself out and says so; the board keeps working on the signed-out paths."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .watchDirectory, area: "splits", title: "What's on, before anything is chosen",
            spec:
                "An empty video slot is a board of what is actually on, not a text box. WatchChooser holds it toolkit-free: the channels this device follows (WatchStore, device-local, listing itself with names and sources even when no source answers), what is live among them with audience and uptime, what is popular now, and the categories to browse — each section compact by default and expanding in place behind one row that says how many it is holding. Typing turns the board into an answer for those words: what they would open leads, and what the sources found live for them follows, while VideoTarget.classify keeps a typed channel behaving exactly as it always did. Both sources are read without an account (Twitch's public GraphQL, YouTube's own web JSON, yt-dlp for a single channel's live state), and an account only ever adds to that, every fetch is the client's to schedule, and a source that cannot answer says which one failed as a row rather than leaving a silent gap. The keys are the ones a text field cannot want — arrows, enter, tab to expand, ctrl+f to follow, esc to step back — because the same box is being typed into. The pane chooser's watch row reports the same fact in one line: WatchSummary names who is live instead of naming the two sites."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .gameCenter, area: "status", title: "The ledger is also a game",
            spec:
                "The month the analytics already fold is scored against a fixed trophy catalog, and Apple's Game Center wears the result. The catalog and every word are Core's (TrophyRoom): each trophy is an identifier shared with App Store Connect, a target, a progress line and Game Center's own 0–100 percent, all read from the same UsageAnalytics merge as the charts — the in-app card and Apple's dashboard can never disagree. The analytics surface carries the trophy case: earned count, the nearest unearned marks as progress bars (the chase, not the shelf), and the road into Apple's own dashboard. Game Center is opt-in (GameCenterConsent) and never a wall: nothing authenticates, so Apple's own \"Sign in to Game Center\" banner never appears over the app, until the person turns it on from the trophy case, and the card renders whole from Core without it, saying plainly that it is off or unavailable. Turning it on presents Apple's sign-in then and there; anyone who had already reported keeps it on, a sign-in that GameKit offers when nobody just asked for one turns it off again instead of raising the banner, and the card offers turning it off whenever it is on. Reporting rides the fetches the app already makes: whenever a haul lands, changed percentages go to GKAchievement and the window's scores to the leaderboards (longest streak, and the month's turns, tokens and tool calls), deduplicated so a refresh costs nothing. Game Center is Apple's account system, so only the Apple clients can answer it; the trophies themselves stay toolkit-free in Core."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .projectBoard, area: "chat list", title: "A project opens as its own board",
            spec:
                "A project names a place work happens — a directory on one machine — and choosing it opens a container, never a composer: the board is that project's chats and nothing else, decided on the exact (profile, directory) pair (ProjectScope.matches), never a path substring, wearing the same rows, sections, live updates and row verbs the whole list wears, and leading with the project's own name. The one thing a launch pad owed — a new chat in that project, pre-aimed at its directory — rides the board's chrome as an offer, so starting work there costs one tap but stays a choice; creation never happens on open. Where the container appears is the client's idiom: the phone opens it from a chat's own menu, which names the project, a desktop sidebar scopes itself from a row's own menu or the p key and wears a clearable banner naming the scope (ProjectScope.banner — project and server both, because two machines can hold a checkout spelled the same) rather than a hidden mode. Leaving the scope restores the whole list unchanged, and conversations that never had a directory form a real scope of their own rather than being unreachable."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .quickAsk, area: "composer", title: "A question skips the setup",
            spec:
                "Some prompts are questions, not work, and a question owes no form: the surface is only a composer and sending is the whole ceremony, but how that composer is reached is the platform's own. A desktop takes a chord from the whole machine, and a window arrives with it over whatever was in front. A phone has no key to take and no window to arrive in — and it already has a prompt box on its front door, which differs from a question in exactly one way: it has a project. So the phone's quick ask is not a sheet summoned over that box but the box's other lane (QuickAskLane), thrown with one small switch inside it: nothing about composing is learned twice, each lane keeps its own draft so the flip loses no words, and what is in hand rides whichever lane is sending. Whichever shape it takes, the lane in force is stated rather than implied — the switch wears the accent when the ask lane is on, the destination chip drops the project a question cannot have, and the empty box says what it is for. A lookup competes with the browser already in the person's hand, so the gesture is also offered outside the app wherever the platform has a place for one — on the phone, the icon's jump list and a Control Center tile, both landing on that same box with the lane already thrown and the keyboard already up, and a press that arrives before the app is standing is parked rather than dropped. The aim is one control naming both halves — which machine answers and which model it answers on — and one action changes either: which server is QuickAskDefaults.target (the machine the last quick ask used while it is still among the servers offered, else the caller's own fallback), and which model is that server's quick-ask memory (QuickAskDefaults.model/effort, filed under an ask context beside the server's own, never inside it). How hard the machine is asked to think is the other half of that memory and is set beside the model on every client: the levels are the picked model's own where the catalog names them and the agent's otherwise (ModelEffort.options), a control with nothing to offer says so rather than reading as a default (ModelEffort.label), and a level the aim can no longer run is handed back to the machine rather than kept as a word the send would not carry. Until a model is picked by hand the aim follows the server, so the first question runs on what a new chat there would have; after one it is the quick ask's own and stays put — pointing lookups at something cheap may never re-aim the project chats, and being asked twice for the same aim is the thing the surface exists to avoid. A model picked on another machine re-aims the whole question there. A send records the server, and the minted conversation is stamped with the aim so it opens on the model the question was asked with. Sending mints an ordinary conversation there with no project directory and the words already on their way, then opens it where conversations open on that client. Afterwards a quick ask is any other chat: it lists, resumes, saves and deletes normally, and its row simply has no project to name rather than pretending one. With no servers the surface says so and offers setup instead of a dead text box. Owing no form is not the same as being able to do nothing, and the surface has three states rather than one. Composing is the chat's own composer, not a smaller one — literally the same prompt box the chat is written in (PromptEditor on the desktops, the front door's own HomeComposerBar on the phone, which answers everything ComposerView does), so it is as tall as what is in it up to the same ceiling the settings set and then scrolls, Enter sends and shift+enter writes the line break under the same preference, a desktop's modal editing is the same engine wearing the same modes on the same border, and the ultracode aura lights around it the same way; a question that needs a paragraph is typed the way a prompt is: whatever the aim can be handed rides with the question — pictures, files, the clipboard's picture, a large paste that becomes the file it already is — each read fully at pick time into a removable chip, capped at 8 MB, and a picture with no words is a send like any other (QuickAskComposition.canSend). What the aim cannot take is never offered: ModelAbilities.resolve narrows the backend's word by the picked model's own, a model the catalog cannot describe is trusted rather than assumed blind, and something already in hand that a model switch makes unreadable is dropped out loud instead of failing on the other machine. The empty state argues for itself rather than showing a placeholder — QuickAskStarters, filtered to what this aim and this device can do, most recently used first, each row the first half of a sentence left in the composer with the caret at its end and never a question the app sent on somebody's behalf — and with them the last few questions asked on that machine (QuickAskRecents), which reopen rather than ask again. How they are laid out is the surface's own — a grid of tiles on a panel that has a screen to spend, a strip of chips above a bar that has none — but the order never is: what the agent can do first, what was already asked behind it. The starters get out of the way the moment there is a question, or something in hand, or a command being named, and come back if it is emptied. Waiting locks the box so nothing is typed into a send already in flight, and a surface with room for a sentence names the machine being waited on; failing shows the diagnosis where the asking happened with the one action that fixes it (NewChatAttempt/NewChatFailure.fix, applied and retried in place, never an alert on whatever a dismissal revealed), and the words, the attachments and the aim all survive it. What is typed survives the surface closing, the lane being thrown, and the aim moving (DraftStore.quickAsk, per server). Every affordance stays the platform's own idiom: a phone offers its camera and takes a drop anywhere on the screen, a desktop takes a drop on the panel and answers chords, and no client invents a wording Core does not carry."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .quickAskClipboard, area: "composer", title: "What was just copied is offered",
            spec:
                "A question summoned from anywhere is most often about something that was just copied, so the empty desktop quick ask reads the clipboard and, when it holds something new, leads with a card for it: what it is (QuickAskClipboard.headline, specific where it was read and plain where it was only detected), a two-line glimpse of copied words with every run of whitespace one space (QuickAskClipboard.preview), and the errands that thing can be worked with on the aimed model (QuickAskClipboard.errands: words to any model, a picture only to one that sees, files only to one that takes files). An errand is the first half of a sentence like a starter: picking it writes the instruction into the composer and hands the clipboard over through the composer's own paste (PasteIntake), so a long paste still becomes the file it already is, and nothing is ever sent by touching it. The errands take the first number chords and the starters follow them. Almost every clipboard holds something copied hours ago, so the card speaks only for news (QuickAskClipboardMemory): the thing copied is offered until it has been worked with (an errand picked, a question sent while it was on offer, or the card set aside with its own close button), and copying anything else makes it news again; closing the surface is not reading it. Where reading the pasteboard raises the system's paste alert, nothing is read to draw the card: news is the pasteboard's own change count and the kind is read from its types, so the card says only what it detected, and the words are read when an errand is picked. A phone's quick ask is the Home composer's lane and pastes with the system's own menu, so iOS has no card."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .summonAnywhere, area: "composer", title: "The question is asked from anywhere",
            spec:
                "A lookup competes with the window already in front of the person, so on a desktop the quick ask is not a shortcut inside this app but a key taken from the whole machine: one chord, pressed in any program, opens the question box (SummonSettings.chord, default SummonChord.standard — Ctrl+Alt+A, inside the two-modifier space a desktop leaves to whatever wants a key from the whole session). It is the only gesture: a chord that already works from every program owes nothing to a second one inside the app, and the bare letter it used to spend there is worth more back in the composer, where a vim hand presses A to append at the end of the line.. The chord is the person's to change and is recorded by pressing it rather than by naming a key, because what a person wants is what their hand already does. Taking a key from the machine can break a program that is not this one, so the chord is judged before it is claimed and the refusal names the thing that would be taken, never a rule: an arrow with shift extends the selection by word in every editor and text field on the machine, an arrow alone moves the caret or the workspace, Tab belongs to the window switcher, a lone editing chord is copy or save everywhere, and a key with no modifier at all belongs to whatever is being typed into. What the machine then did with the chord is a state rather than a boolean, and none of its faces may read as bound: asked for and not yet granted, granted as a different key than the one requested, already answered by something else, and a session with nothing to ask are four different facts (SummonState), and a claimed key states how far it reaches — a desktop that holds the key only while this app runs says so (SummonReach), because a summon that quietly needs the thing it is summoning is worth saying once rather than discovering on the morning it is pressed first. A press reaching a process that is not standing is a launch rather than a dropped keystroke: the app is single-instance, so the summon arrives as an action on the process that already holds the bus name and raises its window before opening the field — a field that opened behind another app's window would take the keystrokes meant for the question with it. Where the platform cannot grant a key at all the surface says which desktop refused and hands over the one line that desktop's own config would need (SummonRecipe, in that desktop's language), which is also the road to a Tailscode that is not running. A phone has no such gesture to give: its share of this is the icon's jump list and the Control Center tile, which quickAsk already carries."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .spokenAsk, area: "composer", title: "A question is asked out loud",
            spec:
                "The quickest way to ask from wherever the person already is, hands full or phone across the room, is to say it, so the quick ask is offered to the platform's own voice and automation layers without the person building anything: an App Shortcut the system lists on its own (Siri, Spotlight, the Shortcuts app, and Vocal Shortcuts, which turns any phrase the person records into a trigger) for asking a question, and another for opening the quick ask box to type in. Asking collects the words before the app opens, by voice when the system is listening and in a text field when it is not, so they are said once; the app then opens on the same box the in-app switch reaches with the lane thrown and sends them as Send would, to the same aim (QuickAskDefaults.target and its model memory), minting the same ordinary conversation. Saying them was the gesture, and a question nobody has to confirm is the point. What is already in the box is never taken along or lost: a box holding a draft, something in hand or a carried conversation keeps all of it, the spoken words join the draft, and Send is left to the person; a send still in flight finishes before they land. The words reach the app in process and never as a link, because anything on the device can open a link and only the person's own words may be sent for them. A question that arrives before the app is standing is parked and delivered once Home exists; one parked behind setup for longer than half a minute lands in the box unsent rather than going off long after it was asked. A gesture that arrives while the app is already in front still lands, rather than waiting for a foreground that never comes."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .reviewPrompt, area: "settings", title: "An App Store review is asked for, never nagged",
            spec:
                "The only moment the app asks for a review is the moment value just landed, and the policy that decides whether to ask is Core's, not a client's: ReviewPromptPolicy counts successes — an agent turn finishing with an answer, in a session on a server this device is connected to, and nothing else — and answers due as a pure function of that count, every ask's own date, the count at the last ask, and now, so the whole doctrine is tested without touching UserDefaults. The first ask is early, due the moment a second success lands, because value delivered twice already argues for itself. Every ask after the first waits for both a fortnight and three fresh successes since the one before it, and no ask is ever due once three already sit inside the trailing year, which is Apple's own ceiling on how often the system will even show the sheet — the policy enforces it itself rather than trusting a dismissal it is never told about. The ask is asked roughly a second and a half after the success, so the success's own feedback lands first, and only while the app is foreground-active, a window exists, and nothing — a purchase sheet, an alert, onboarding — sits over the content; a success recorded while nobody could be asked is asked instead the next time the app comes to the foreground, if it is still due then. The ask itself is the platform's own store-review call (AppStore.requestReview(in:)) — nothing renders a custom prompt, and a dismissal the system never reports back is not something the policy pretends to know. A platform without a store to review answers notApplicable, and the policy is never told to ask there."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .proUnlock, area: "settings", title: "The unlock, and the one gate it answers",
            spec:
                "Tailscode is GPL-3.0 and every screen is in the free app, so the purchase is a convenience and a way to fund the work rather than a wall: ProOffer in Core holds the whole policy and every word of it — the product identifiers, what the free copy holds (one server, ProOffer.freeServerLimit), what Pro adds, the sentence said at the gate, and the tip jar that unlocks nothing at all. The store record is shared across the Apple clients and so are the product ids, so one purchase covers iPhone and Mac on the same Apple Account and each client only has to ask StoreKit what it already knows. The gate is on ADDING a server past the free limit, never on keeping one already configured, and it never refuses without opening the window that answers it. Nothing asks on launch, nothing asks on a timer, and no surface is hidden behind the unlock. A client with no store to buy from, or a build with no receipt to verify, is simply unlocked rather than pretending to sell something it cannot deliver."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .supporterInvitation, area: "settings", title: "One invitation to fund the work, earned by use",
            spec:
                "Most people run one machine and never meet the server gate, so they never learn the unlock exists; the answer is one dismissible invitation, earned by use rather than by time. SupporterInvitation in Core counts turns that finished well and is due at ten, never while Pro is held, and never again once set aside or bought (settle). The copy is Core's — the title, ProOffer.pitch plus the one-purchase, iPhone-and-Mac, unlimited-servers line, and a primary action carrying the store's price once it has answered — and it keeps ProOffer's stance whole: no wall, no trial, nothing on launch, and a second ask never happens. A client with nothing to sell renders nothing."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoForge, area: "splits", title: "A video is asked for and watched being made",
            spec:
                "A person describes a video on any client and watches it be made on the machine with the card. Everything but the drawing is Core's. Where the renderer lives is a ForgeEndpoint — a host and ComfyUI's own 8188 read through the same HostAddress parser the agent connection uses, kept in ForgeStore, and probed with PortReachability rather than an HTTP request so \"nothing is listening\" is told apart from \"that machine is asleep\" and \"that name does not resolve\" (ForgeEndpoint.sentence names each). What is rendered is a ForgeRecipe — prompt, negative, a frame size that is a multiple of 32, seconds, frame rate, seed — on the one transformer this app renders with, LTX-2.5 distilled on its eight measured sigmas at a guidance of one, which is the recipe Lightricks ships for 2.5 and the only one verified on the box; the undistilled checkpoint is a training base whose own pipeline needs a refinement LoRA that does not exist for 2.5, and it is not offered — and ForgeGraph turns it into the twenty-eight-node two-pass graph verified on the box, encoding the invariants as code rather than as comments: length is seconds times rate plus one, the first pass samples at half the asked-for size and the latent upsampler doubles it back, and ForgeGraph.problems refuses to post a graph whose links do not resolve. ForgeClient runs it: one client id shared by the POST and the websocket or the frames land on somebody else's socket, a 45-second budget on the first request because the box is socket-activated and takes about twelve seconds to answer cold, every frame type parsed by ForgeEvent including the null-node executing frame that is how a finished render announces itself, /interrupt to cancel, and the file resolved out of history to a /view URL — with a salvage loop so a phone that locked its screen mid-render still gets its clip. Progress is the node census from progress_state (finished over total), never the sampler's step counter, which restarts between the two passes and would fill, empty and fill again. ForgeJob is the state machine (drafting, submitting, queued, running, done, failed, cancelled) and owns every word on screen — title, subtitle, detail, badge, elapsed, which pass is working — and ForgeBoard lays it out as four sections every client draws identically: the renderer and whether it answered, the render in hand with its bar, the settings as walkable rows, and the clips already made. A failure is never silent: ForgeFailure carries a sentence naming the machine and, where it can, the fix, and ForgeClient.reason normalises anything foreign so no client ever prints an error object. What each client owes is the drawing — rows, a text field, a video player for ForgeAsset.url(on:) — and nothing else. How the surface is reached and how the machine behind it is chosen are capabilities of their own (forgeEntry, forgeSetup), because a renderer nobody can find and a box nobody can open are the two ways this feature is present and unusable."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .forgeEntry, area: "app", title: "Video is a first-class action, opened over the work",
            spec:
                "A render is a task you start, watch and collect — not a place you work — so it is reached like an action and presented like one. The control is first-class and visible with the app’s other primary ones rather than buried in a settings menu or a menu bar: one title, one SF Symbol and one text glyph everywhere (ForgeEntryPoint), a tooltip that promises something different before a renderer exists than after, and a live mark while a render is out (ForgeEntryPoint.activity) so somebody who closed the surface can still see the other machine working. Opening it opens a modal over the window — a sheet on the Mac, a modal transient-for window on Linux, a presented screen on the phone — never a pane in the tiling grid: a surface nobody types into for four minutes does not earn half a transcript’s width, and it left a pane behind afterwards. The transcript is untouched behind it and comes back whole when the modal closes. Every desktop opens it at the same size (ForgeSurface.preferredWidth/Height, with a floor) and closes it the same way (ForgeSurface.dismissTitle, and Escape where the platform means Escape). The one thing dismissal may never do is stop the render: the job and its socket live above the surface the way ForgeRunner already holds them on iOS, so closing is closing a window rather than cancelling minutes of somebody else’s card — and while a render is out the surface says exactly that (ForgeSurface.dismissNote). Reopening finds the same render where it was. A keyboard route stays alongside the control rather than replacing it."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .forgeSetup, area: "servers", title: "The renderer is set up the way a server is",
            spec:
                "Pointing at the renderer is held to the standard adding a server is held to, and every word and rule of it is Core’s (ForgeSetup). The surface states what this machine’s own tailnet address is before asking for anybody else’s, because a probe from a machine with no tailnet fails identically however right the address is. The address is not typed blind where it can be found: ForgeSweep asks every machine the agent scan would ask (TailnetScanner.scannableDevices, so phones and peers nobody has seen in ten minutes are skipped) whether anything answers on ComfyUI’s 8188, using PortReachability rather than an HTTP probe — TailnetScanner itself cannot serve here, since its probe discards everything that is not an agent, which is every ComfyUI there has ever been. Each machine is asked by MagicDNS name first and by one address behind it, and only a name that failed to resolve is worth trying the second address on (ForgeSweep.step). The wait is TailnetRadar’s dial, unchanged: every machine takes its stable place before a probe lands, answers light it, and what never answered leaves the dial rather than reading as a maybe (ForgeSweepReading). A machine a scan found is adopted already checked. Anything typed is read by the same HostAddress parser the agent connection uses, so a bare name, a MagicDNS name, host:port, a URL or a whole line pasted out of ComfyUI’s terminal all work, the supplied port is said out loud (ForgeSetup.hint), and the three refusals are ForgeEndpoint.complaint’s. What is typed is checked before it is saved, and a failure explains itself in a sentence naming the fix rather than a raw error: ConnectDiagnosis.forge maps the three verdicts onto the same value the server flow uses — off the tailnet and a name that will not resolve are literally the server flow’s own sentences, while a port refusing on a machine that is up is the renderer’s own and hands over the line that starts ComfyUI bound to loopback and the tailnet address and nowhere else (ConnectDiagnosis.Fix.copyCommand, ForgeSetup.startCommand). One rule is the renderer’s rather than the server flow’s: a machine that said nothing may still be the right machine, because ComfyUI is socket-activated and stops itself when idle — so silence and a refusal may be saved anyway with the reason stated (ForgeSetup.secondary), while a name this device cannot resolve may not, since that files an address that can never work. The answer is remembered: ForgeStore keeps every renderer this device has pointed at, most recent first, named the way the tailnet names it, so the second time is a tap; a name learned later replaces a blank one, forgetting one stops sending work to it, and the machine in force wears a word (ForgeRenderer.badge). The picker is the way to change the machine the shared checklist for images and video checks, and returns to that checklist once a machine is saved; it no longer draws what the machine can make. A client owes a text field, a list, a radar and buttons. It decides nothing and writes no sentence."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .mediaReadiness, area: "servers", title: "Images and video are proven ready before the first render",
            spec:
                "Images and video are ready before they are used, not at the first render that fails. Before the first render, and whenever the renderer is saved or changed, the client asks ComfyUI what it has (MediaDoctor: /system_stats for the card and version, /object_info for node classes, /models/<folder> for the files, with the loaders’ own choices as the fallback for a ComfyUI that predates it) and compares it with what each lane submits (MediaRequirements, pinned to the graphs by tests): five files for the LTX-2.5 distilled video lane, three each for the Qwen Quality, Qwen Turbo and Klein picture engines, and the node classes every graph names. The answer is a SetupReport of capability media, with stable check ids (media.comfy, media.gpu, media.video.nodes, media.video.models, media.image.nodes, media.image.models, media.enhancer, one row per missing file) and facts that are names and numbers; MediaReading writes every word. A missing file is named by its role and size (“The video model is missing: 42 GB, ltx-2.5-22b-distilled-transformer-bf16.safetensors”) with the one hf download line that fetches it, never by a node’s error; a file in a subfolder is said to be misplaced; a file nobody has published would carry no command and an unpublished fact. Why ComfyUI cannot be reached is not one sentence: nothing listening on the port, running but for its own machine only (told apart only on the machine it runs on), asleep or off the tailnet, a name that does not resolve, something else answering, and starting (a socket that accepts while the process loads, asked again every few seconds, never a failure). Video and pictures share one answer per machine (MediaReadiness: a thirty-second cache, one look in flight however many ask), the picture side’s ImageGenHealth is that report in its own shape, and a render that cannot work is refused in the doctor’s words before it is posted (ForgeClient, ImageGenRunner), the same words that replace a raw node error when a post is rejected. Every road to what a machine can make opens the one shared checklist for capability media, led by where the person came from (SetupFocus): the video button on a machine that cannot render, the render button’s “See what is missing” (ForgeBoard.begin answers .checklist), the studio’s machine pill in both lanes, and the server screen’s Capabilities row. MediaSetupSource serves the doctor’s report to SetupDesk for the renderer the person uses, MediaSetupReader writes every word, and every fix is a command to copy: the button copies the next missing file’s command in order and then says Render or Open the studio. The licences are said once each in the row that needs them (LTX-2.5 and FLUX Klein are gated on Hugging Face: accept the licence while signed in and run hf auth login; Qwen Image 2.1 is for personal and research use only). The start command ComfyUI is given binds loopback and the tailnet address only and starts nothing when Tailscale has no address (ForgeSetup.startCommand), because an unauthenticated ComfyUI must not listen on every network the machine is on. The renderer is guessed, not typed: the agent server’s own host on 8188 (MediaEndpointPlan), asked silently when the surface first opens, filed against that server (ForgeStore.endpoint(forProfile:)) only once it answers. The prompt helper is optional and never blocks. The recipe a stranger follows to install ComfyUI and every public model file is docs/media-setup.md, performed by scripts/media-setup.sh."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .forgeHistory, area: "splits", title: "Every clip made is still findable",
            spec:
                "A render costs minutes of another machine's card and the file it wrote lives on that machine, so what this device keeps is the receipt: ForgeStore.history holds a capped list of ForgeEntry — the prompt, the settings, the seed and the three fields that fetch the file back — filed the moment a job stops, whether it produced a clip or a reason it did not. That is enough to list what was made, play any of it again through ForgeAsset.url(on:) with whatever endpoint is current rather than a URL that went stale when the address changed, and — the whole point of showing a seed — ask for the same clip again or the same clip slightly different: ForgeBoard.reuse puts an old recipe back in the draft. The list is device-local like the archive and the watchlist, compacts to four rows behind one expander that says how many it is holding, and an empty one says it is empty rather than vanishing. A client owes the rows and a way to remove one; the order, the wording, the badge and the cap are Core's."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoLane, area: "composer", title: "The prompt box has a video lane",
            spec:
                "A video is asked for in words, and the words are typed where words are already typed: the front door's prompt box carries video as its third lane beside chat and the quick ask, so describing a clip costs no navigation. The lanes are Core's (QuickAskLane: order, word, symbol, placeholder, spoken, sendLabel), and the lane in force is stated rather than implied — the switch wears the lane's own word and symbol, the accent whenever the box is anywhere but chat, the empty box says what this lane is for, and the send control changes face and name (Render, never Send), because the video lane spends minutes of another machine's card and a send nobody meant must be impossible to miss. The flip is a tap on the switch — one walk through the three lanes — and, where the platform has a finger, a horizontal swipe on the box itself: the bar follows the drag with weight (ComposerLaneSwipe.offset), commits only past a deliberate distance (ComposerLaneSwipe.threshold), lands with the selection haptic and a spring, and a drag that stays short springs back having changed nothing. A desktop has no swipe and does not fake one: the same three lanes sit on its composer's own strip as buttons, and because each lane on a desk is a surface of its own, a press is a door — chat is the pane it was pressed in and wears the accent, ask opens the question window the summon chord opens, video opens the forge — while the words in the box stay the conversation's. The lane's tools appear only while the lane does: the destination chip names the renderer the way the chat lane names its project — or says none is set up and opens the setup — and under the box the walkable settings ride as chips (QuickAskLane.videoChips), each cycled with the same tap the board answers, plus the road into the full surface. The words in the box are the forge's own draft (the recipe prompt), so the lane and the video surface can never hold two different prompts — typed here is typed there, and both survive a restart with the recipe. Sending is ForgeRunner's begin: a configured renderer starts the render and opens the video surface over the work so the first seconds of waiting are watched rather than trusted; a missing renderer opens the same surface leading with its setup instead of failing the send. The lane resets to chat when the front door is next stood up — a composer that quietly stays in video mode is how a prompt meant for the agent burns a render — while the words, like every draft, survive the flip."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoPromptHelper, area: "composer", title: "A small model writes the clip's caption",
            spec:
                "LTX-2.5 was trained on captions of clips — one paragraph reporting a shot as it plays, camera first, present tense, the sound named — and three nouns typed into the box get a clip that holds still. So the forge makes the same offer the image studio makes, through the same machinery: the helper is the one `ImageGenStore.helper` files (found by `ImageGenHelperFinder.survey` near the renderer, picked from the same grouped list with the current one marked, switchable off without being forgotten), `ImageGenRewriter` streams the paragraph into the same card (`ImageGenRewriteDraft`, `ImageGenRewriteWords`: who is writing and how many words so far; use these words, keep mine, write again; one line for what to change), and only the question differs: `ForgeBrief.system` asks for the caption of the finished clip — the shot and the camera move, the subject's motion in order, the setting and light, one closing sentence on what is heard with any spoken line in quotes, eighty to a hundred and sixty words, one continuous shot — and `ForgeRewriteContext` tells it what the brief does not say: the clip's own length so nothing needs a cut, that it opens on a supplied picture (describe what moves, not the still) or continues an earlier clip (what happens next, not a new scene), the shape when one was chosen by hand, the sound the person already wrote so it is not written twice, and the avoid list. The shape it answers with is followed by the size chip only where nobody chose one (`ForgeBoard.follow(size:)`, `ForgeSize.following`). Taking the paragraph puts it in the box where it can still be edited and keeps the typed sentence one press away; nothing is ever rendered on the person's behalf."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoLivePreview, area: "composer", title: "The clip is watched as it is made",
            spec:
                "A render is a minute of somebody's card and a bar over a glyph tells nothing about whether that minute is going right. ComfyUI decodes the sampler's latent after every step — the first frame of the clip, through the model's own latent-to-colour projection — and sends it down the same socket the progress comes on as a binary frame; `ForgeClient` gathers the pieces with `ImageGenPreviewAssembler` (a socket under Linux Foundation hands a message over in sixteen-kilobyte parts) and folds each whole one into the job as `ForgeEvent.sketched`, `ForgeJob.sketch` carries the newest, and the stage draws it in place of the phase glyph — full width, the newest frame replacing the last without a rebuild, captioned as a sketch with the pass and the sampler's own step (`ForgeWords.sketchCaption`) so it is never mistaken for the clip. Eight frames arrive in the first pass and three sharper ones in the second, so the sketch is seen to resolve. The finished clip arrives under the last sketch — a crossfade once, from the sketch to the player as the file loads, with the sketch let go when it is over — rather than in place of it; reduced motion shows the player at once; a stop or a failure clears the sketch; a server started without `--preview-method` sends no frames, in which case the stage shows exactly what it showed before and nothing is inferred. The sketch is the machine's, never re-encoded or kept."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoFirstFrame, area: "composer", title: "A clip can open on a picture",
            spec:
                "A clip that starts from a picture is the bridge between the two things this app can make, and it is one decision with three sources (`ForgeFrame`): a file on this device, which the render puts on the machine first (`ForgeClient.place`, the same `/upload/image` road the image studio's references take) and names by what came back; a picture the machine already keeps, named in its own directory so no byte travels; and the end of a clip already made, opened on the machine (`LoadVideo` on the clip's annotated name, `GetVideoComponents`, `ImageFromBatch` clamped to the last frame) so one clip continues into the next. `ForgeGraph` carries the verified image-to-video shape: the picture compressed as the model expects (`LTXVPreprocess`) and written into the first frame of each pass's latent by `LTXVImgToVideoInplace` — held at 0.7 on the first pass so the motion can begin and pinned at 1.0 on the refinement — and the prompt the encoder is given opens with the verified line that names the picture as the first frame (`ForgeRecipe.renderedPrompt`), so a description that starts mid-motion does not fight the conditioning. The size follows the picture (`ForgeSize.nearest`) or the clip it continues unless a size was chosen by hand, because a photograph cropped to a frame it never had is not what anybody meant. The chip under the words names the picture and offers to replace or drop it; every kept clip offers Continue it (`ForgeBoard.extend`: the same words to edit, the same shape, a fresh seed); the image studio's finished picture offers Animate this, which opens the forge holding that picture as the first frame. A graph that would need an upload nobody made refuses to post in words (`ForgeGraph.problems`)."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoSound, area: "composer", title: "The sound is said, and heard",
            spec:
                "LTX-2.5 renders a soundtrack with every clip whether or not anybody asked for one, and a surface that never mentions it leaves a person surprised by a voice. So the sound is a thing to describe: `ForgeRecipe.sound` is its own line under the words (`ForgeField.sound`, placeholder `ForgeWords.soundPlaceholder`), sent to the model as the closing sentence of the prompt after the caption (`renderedPrompt`), remembered with the recipe and shown in its summary, and left empty to let the model decide — the helper is told when it is written so the caption does not say it twice, and asked to close with a sound sentence when it is not. A playing clip wears the mark (`ForgeWords.soundOnMark` / `soundOffMark`) so it is known to have a soundtrack before the speakers say so, and the one key that mutes it is stated beside the mark. The avoid list is told the truth about itself: the distilled model runs at a guidance of one, where a negative prompt changes nothing, and its row says so (`ForgeWords.negativeIgnoredHint`)."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoEstimate, area: "composer", title: "A render says how long it should take",
            spec:
                "A clip costs pixels times frames, and the card works through them at a rate that is the machine's and the model's rather than the clip's — so `ForgeClock` keeps one rate per model, seconds per megapixel-frame, moved halfway toward each finished render (measured from the machine's first frame about the job to its last, never from the queue or the wake), and prices the next recipe from it. It is kept on the device (`ForgeStore.clock`) and read back into the board (`ForgeBoard.learned`), so the second clip on a fresh install already has a figure. The figure is worn in two places and said as \u{201c}about\u{201d} in both: under the draft before anybody presses the button (`ForgeBoard.expectation`, in the render row's own subtitle) and beside the bar while it runs (`ForgeJob.remaining`: the estimate less the clock, and nothing at all once the render has outrun it, because a bar still moving over \u{201c}0 s left\u{201d} is a lie a person catches at once). Nothing is said on a model that has never been measured; a guess dressed as a fact is the one thing this vocabulary refuses."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .videoLight, area: "composer", title: "The video button shows where the render is",
            spec:
                "The way in to video says where the render is, as light round its own edge — and says it the same way on every desk, because the only person who watches it is somebody who is looking at something else. `ForgeLight` (Core) reads a job into seven faces: dark, waking, waiting behind other jobs on the shared machine, rendering with how far along, saving, a clip that landed that nobody has seen, and a render that broke that nobody has read about. Each wears the vocabulary the rest of the app speaks (`ForgeLight.tone`, `ForgeLight.motion`): a wake sweeps as a comet whose tail is the colour of waiting, painted work is a live arc that fills from the top, clockwise, with a light travelling along what has been done so a render between two steps still reads as alive, a queue holds still, saving breathes, and a clip that landed or a render that broke holds perfectly still and stays lit until the person has looked at it (the clip on a stage, the failure on a card), because an ending is news until it is read and stillness is how a reader tells a stopped render from a slow one. `ForgeRing` is the arithmetic, read from absolute time: strokes are fractions of the way round the control's edge from the top centre, never crossing it, and `ForgeRing.follow` eases the arc toward the machine's latest word because progress arrives in jumps and a ring that jumps with it reads as a fault. Reduced motion drops the comet, the travelling light and the breathing and keeps everything else. Words for a screen reader and the tooltip are `ForgeLight.spoken`, since a ring says nothing to somebody who cannot see it. A client decides only how the stroke is painted."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .voiceCall, area: "chat", title: "A call lets you speak to a chat",
            spec:
                "A call is an ear for a chat that already exists: the person talks, a voice model on the server\u{2019}s GPU (VoiceChat, reached through voice-bridge) listens and writes down what it hears as it hears it, and every final transcript of at least three words is sent to the agent the conversation already has through the app\u{2019}s own send path (the queue, the pending ink, the marks and the Live Activity behave as if it had been typed). Nothing is spoken back, on purpose: the gate of 2026-10-16 measured the model as an excellent ear and a poor mouth (it does not call tools reliably and ignores instructions to be silent), so its speech and captions are muted at the gateway, it has no tools, and the client never plays its audio, shows its words or reads a state of speaking. What it heard is shown on the call strip as it arrives and is sometimes wrong, so the agent gets what was heard. Everything toolkit-free is Core\u{2019}s and never main-actor: VoiceCallSession owns one call over three seams a client implements or inherits (VoiceTransport, with URLSessionVoiceTransport; VoiceAudioCapture, PCM16 24 kHz mono; VoiceAudioPlayback, which carries only the chimes), sends hello, forwards the microphone, answers every dispatch sent or refused from the app\u{2019}s own send path, repeats the agent frame every five seconds, redials a lost line after 1, 2 and 4 seconds before ending lost, and never leaves capture or playback running once the call has ended. SpokenDigest turns the conversation the client already holds into the agent frame by rule, with no model, for a gateway that asks for it. VoiceCallReading writes every word and VoiceCallFace is the one picture of the call on every desk \u{2014} connecting sweeps, listening and the agent working breathe, hearing is lit steady, and degraded and ended hold perfectly still, degraded in the danger tone; the speaking face is kept for a model that talks again and is never reached \u{2014} while the agent working keeps its own ActivityKind face on the chat\u{2019}s status line. The strip is one caption line (what you said) and the agent\u{2019}s account; when the agent finishes while the person is quiet the snapshot\u{2019}s chime counter rises and the client plays its chime and haptic. VoiceCallDesk holds the one call a process may be in above every surface (starting a call from any chat ends the one running and the earlier surface reads why from its snapshot), feeds the agent\u{2019}s account only to the call\u{2019}s own chat and keeps each server\u{2019}s reach fresh while a surface asks. Availability is VoiceReach, read by VoiceReachProbe from the gateway\u{2019}s health per server in two seconds, with the reason a call cannot start written out (no gateway, the model loading, the GPU busy, a gateway or an app too old, a password wanted); the demo hosts get a scripted call with no network (VoiceDemo). Calls ship as a beta (BetaFeature.voiceCall): the card says it listens and types, does not talk back, mishears now and then, can split a request at a pause, is English only and needs an NVIDIA GPU with about 64 GB, and Copy report carries the gateway\u{2019}s address and version (VoiceCallReport). A client draws and invents nothing: the capture and the chimes over its audio stack, the keep-awake and audio session, the entry point (VoiceCallEntryPoint) and the call surface."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .setupChecklist, area: "settings", title: "A capability that is not ready shows what it needs, and does it",
            spec:
                "A capability the machine has not got ready (voice calls, and images and video) is never a toast that says to run a command: every road that would have said so opens one checklist for that capability on that machine, and it is drawn from Core\u{2019}s SetupChecklist alone. The machine proves what it can do (SetupKit: checks with stable ids and facts, fixes as jobs it does or one command that needs the person\u{2019}s rights) and SetupReading writes every word in the person\u{2019}s language from the id and the facts, falling back to the daemon\u{2019}s own detail for an id the app has never seen. A row is a glyph in the activity vocabulary (breathing while a job runs, perfectly still when settled, tone per state), a title, one line of facts and its fix: a job is a button that becomes a determinate bar reading x of y GB with the time left and a Cancel, a command is a monospace block with Copy and the reason it needs the person, a blocker is its explanation, and a machine that can never do it says so once (Not possible on this machine: it has no NVIDIA GPU with 64 GB) and stays out of the way. The headline is one line (Voice calls are ready, Needs one thing, Working: downloading the voice model, 31%) and there is exactly one primary button at the foot: Set up voice runs every job fix in order until a command or a blocker is reached, Copy command hands over the one command, Try a call when everything is proven. A checklist may be opened for the part of a capability the person came from (SetupFocus), which changes its order and its headline and never what is true, and may name the machine it checks with a Change action that opens that machine’s own picker (SetupPlace); a row may be optional (Optional · …) and never holds the checklist back, so images and video lead with video or pictures and fold the other lane into one optional row. SetupDesk, an actor and never main-actor, fetches reports (the voice gateway\u{2019}s GET /setup; a host with no answering gateway is a synthetic report whose one open check is the voice-bridge install command), polls only while a surface watches and stops when it closes, starts a fix and follows its job by id so a relaunch finds the open job in the next report, cancels, and chains. A demo host answers a scripted setup in which every state is seen with no machine. The voice checklist wears the beta mark. A client draws and invents nothing: the rows, the bar, the command block and the button, in a sheet on the phone, a sheet on the Mac and a dialog window on Linux, with reduced motion dropping movement and keeping every word."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .serverCapabilities, area: "settings", title: "A server says what it can do",
            spec:
                "The server screen carries one Capabilities section: a row for each capability the machine reports as available, with a state chip (Ready, Needs setup, Setting up) and one line of what is true, and a tap opens the checklist behind it. A capability whose machine can never do it is not shown, and a machine whose gateway does not answer gets one quiet row that offers to add it (Add voice calls) rather than a red state, and images and video are a row (Images and video, a chip and one line) wherever the renderer answers or this device has used it, else one quiet Add image and video generation row where the machine is up and no ComfyUI answers, because a server that was never meant to hear is not broken. The rows are SetupCapabilityRow, built from the same snapshots the checklist reads, so the section and the sheet can never disagree; the section watches only while the screen is on show."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .autoResume, area: "chat", title: "A window that closed opens by itself",
            spec:
                "A message a spent quota stopped is held and sent again the moment the provider\u{2019}s window resets, without anybody being at the machine \u{2014} the same for a Claude Code chat and an opencode one, because the whole decision is Core\u{2019}s and neither server is asked to do anything it cannot. AutoResume is that decision and it is arithmetic on a time the provider stated, never a hopeful timer: the fire moment is the reset plus AutoResume.grace, a wall that states no reset (a prepaid balance opens when somebody pays, not on a clock) is refused in words rather than guessed at, and a reset past AutoResume.horizon is declined because holding a message written now to send it on Friday is not a convenience. What may be resumed is exactly what was never answered \u{2014} a send this device is still holding, or a turn that reached the server and produced nothing at all (ChatMessage.isAnswerless, via AutoResume.mayAskAgain); a turn that had begun writing is never asked again by a clock at any attempt count, because half an answer is a thing a person reads before deciding what comes next. At the fire moment the gauges are re-read rather than trusted (AutoResume.recheck): a wall that lifted sends, a wall still standing with a newer reset re-plans onto it, and one with nothing new to say falls onto a bounded backoff \u{2014} four tries, then it stops and says so, with the words still on the row. Every plan is written to ResumeStore with the message whole, so a phone killed at midnight still knows at eight what it was holding and why; a plan whose moment passed while nothing was running goes stale rather than firing late, and is reported (ResumeReading.missed) rather than sent into a conversation that has moved on. The wait obeys the activity doctrine \u{2014} it is settled, so it holds perfectly still and only its countdown moves \u{2014} and every word is ResumeReading\u{2019}s: the row\u{2019}s caption, the band\u{2019}s short form, the notification, the one way out (\u{201C}Don\u{2019}t wait\u{201D}), the setting\u{2019}s own title, and a sentence for each of the five reasons nothing is being waited for. A client owes the clock, the row and the setting; it decides nothing."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .rateAndShare, area: "settings", title: "The store is one row away, on purpose",
            spec:
                "The review prompt asks only when value just landed and carries no written word, so a person who wants to say something — or to hand the app to a colleague — needs a door they open themselves. Settings ▸ About carries two rows: Rate opens the store's own review form for this app, and Share hands the store listing to the platform's share sheet beside one sentence saying what the app is (AppStoreLinks), so the message is never a bare link. Neither row counts toward or resets the review policy: they are doors, not asks. A platform with no store has no such door and says so rather than showing a dead row."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .midgarPortal, area: "settings", title: "The other Midgar apps are one card away",
            spec:
                "Every Midgar app carries exactly one doorway into the portal, a full-screen honeycomb of the publisher\u{2019}s other apps, and it is the first thing in Settings: one card, 116 points tall, that opens the portal itself when it is tapped or clicked, with no row, header, footer or separator of its own. The card is MidgarKit\u{2019}s `MidgarEntryView`, and the app registers its own lowercased bundle identifier as a URL scheme and lists every Midgar scheme in `LSApplicationQueriesSchemes`, which is how the portal knows what is installed. A client that cannot link MidgarKit has no door and says so rather than showing a dead card."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .worldClock, area: "chrome", title: "A world clock lives at the foot of the sidebar",
            spec:
                "The time is always in view and the world is one touch away. At the foot of the sidebar, beside the Update chip, sits a small capsule with the time here (HH:mm, or 12-hour by choice) and a horizon glyph that is the sky at this desk: a sun climbing its arc by day, a moon crossing by night, warm at the edges (SkyGlyph). It ticks once a minute, aligned to the minute (WorldClockReading.nextTick), and a glyph that is not moving is drawn once. Hovering it opens a glance that stays while the pointer is on the chip or the glance: the date and week, then one row for here and one for every pinned city (WorldClockPreferences, tailscode.worldClock) with the offset and whether it is today, yesterday or tomorrow there, a strip of that city's own day from night to day with its twilight ramps and a dot at its local moment (Daylight, Solar: sunrise and sunset computed from the city's coordinates, no network), and its time; then the machines the app talks to, each with how far its clock is from this one (ServerClockLedger in the Kit reads the Date header of the answers the app already gets, so it costs nothing; MachineClocks states in sync, a few seconds either way, or the minutes behind or ahead, and a machine far enough off carries a sentence saying chats it dates will read early or in the future); then the quota windows about to reopen with a countdown (ClockResets over the same QuotaHolding the quota board uses). Clicking it, or Enter or Space on it, opens Meridian, a sheet with one lane per place on the viewer's own day laid out as a line (MeridianBoard: DayAxis in real minutes, so a day with a clock change in it is 23 or 25 hours long), each lane shaded from night to day with the sun at its solar noon, the quota resets marked on the viewer's lane, and a line that is dragged, clicked or moved with the arrow keys to any minute of the day: every lane then reads its own time and day at that moment, the head reads the moment in the viewer's zone and how far from now it is, and Now returns to following the clock. The hours when everybody pinned is awake are a band across the lanes with the sentence that says them (Awake, MeridianBoard.golden and insight), with awake being 07:00-23:00 local by convention and said as awake and never as available. The moment can be copied as ISO 8601 with offset, as UTC, or as a unix epoch, and Notify me at that moment (ClockAlarm, ClockAlarmBook, tailscode.worldClock.alarms) raises a notification when the app is running at that minute, naming how late it is if the app was closed across it; a quota reset mark offers the same. Places are managed where they are shown: the last row under the lanes is Add a place, which opens a field whose matches (MeridianPlaces.results over WorldCities.search, accents and case ignored, with aliases) are rows that read by their clock — the city, its country and offset, the time it is there now and how far that is from here — with the places most people watch offered as rows before anything is typed (MeridianPlaces.suggestions), a count of how many of the eight are held (PlacesStatus), and a plain sentence when nothing matches or the list is full; a lane is moved up or down and removed from its own controls, which show on hover and on keyboard focus. The time format is a labelled three-way choice that shows what each option would write (ClockStyle.example: 14:07, 2:07 PM, and what Follow system follows), never a menu button that says only its current word. Nothing is pinned by default and an empty glance offers the first four popular places. The surface opens at the size its own content needs, bounded by the screen, and no label on it is ever cut short at any type size: a column takes the width of its longest member rather than a number chosen for one size of type, and what cannot fit on a line wraps. Everything a client says comes from Core: it decides only how a lane and a strip are painted. A click on the track glides the line to its minute in one short motion; reduced motion lands it at once, and nothing else on the surface moves."),
    ]
}
